  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1119 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nAn anomalous 590dam ridge continues to dominate the \nweather pattern across the Mississippi Valley bringing warm \nafternoons in the mid 70s to lower 80s and chilly mornings in the \n40s to mid 50s. A very dry airmass remains fixed over the region \nwith PWAT values around 0.5-0.7\", nearing the 25th percentile for \nthis time of the year. Such PWATs will not be able to sustain any \nprecipitation through the workweek. Fire weather concerns will \nalso persist as dry air mixes down each afternoon with dewpoints \nin the 40s to lower 50s. We ask you to please continue to adhere \nto local burn bans, properly dispose of cigarettes and matches, \nand avoid using equipment that creates sparks. \n\nBy midday Tuesday, the aforementioned ridge will finally begin to \nbreak down as as shortwave troughing begins to push over the \nSouthern Plains. Upper-level southwest flow will begin to build over \nthe Mississippi Valley. Little to no change to the forecast will be \nwitnessed as temperatures remain above normal and dry conditions \npersist. Enough moisture may be able to filter in to increase \nrelative humidity values by a small margin, hopefully helping to \nalleviate fire weather concerns. However, local burn bans will remain \nin effect due to our ongoing dry spell. Our next shot at \nprecipitation returns Halloween weekend as a subtropical jet begins \nto strengthen over the Southern Plains. Forecast PWATs increase to \naround 1.5-1.7\", nearing the 97th percentile for late October. \nLatest LREF guidance has a 40-55% chance of exceeding 0.5\" and a 20-\n30% chance of exceeding 1\" of rainfall. Any rainfall will be \nwelcomed as extreme drought (D3) and exceptional drought (D4) \ncurrently exist over portions of the Mid-South.\n\nAEH",
